// A METHOD cannot return an interface object either. Roadmap B53.
//
// A free function returning `dynamic Trait` has always been refused: the object BORROWS the value
// behind it, so returning one hands back a reference to storage the call is about to drop. A method
// was not refused — the same spelling, the same stated reason, two different answers.
//
// Found while closing B47, by CHECKING before copying the free-function arm across to stage-1.
// Copying it would have closed one divergence by opening another in the opposite direction, and the
// only way to know that was to run the program rather than assume the two arms belonged together.
//
// **It needed a ruling rather than a fix, and the measurement is why.** The method version RUNS
// correctly — including across a call that reuses the stack — because an aggregate parameter is
// `byval`: the copy lives in the CALLER's frame and outlives the call. So stage-0 may be over-strict
// on free functions rather than unsound on methods, and one experiment is not a memory model.
//
// Ruled the conservative way. Between relaxing a safety refusal on the strength of a single run and
// making both refuse, the region model states its own direction: the failure is memory, never a
// dangling pointer. Nothing in this repository returns a `dynamic` from anywhere, so agreeing costs
// nothing today — and it would cost a compatibility promise once 1.0 ships.
interface Greet { function hi(self) -> Int }

class Box { tag: Int }

implement Greet for Box {
    function (self) hi() -> Int { return self.tag; }
}

function (self: Box) lend() -> dynamic Greet {
    return self;
}

let b: Box = Box { tag: 1 };
print(b.lend().hi());
